ly said in terms  of wrestling and I know from Jim  sterling because he talks about it in  terms of video games but when the  business is hot you can do no wrong when  the business is cold you can do no right  now  gonna pick that apart if you've never  heard it but the short version of that  is when you're making money you assume  everything is great we're whatever we're  doing is validated because obviously  we're making money so people are fine  with it and that is when you tend to  make your mistakes that is when you do  your damage but because the effects of  the damage you're doing while you're  making money  don't become evident until a little  while later when you start losing money  by the time you start correcting you're  like oh we need to fix this and do  things right people have already cooled  on you now the business is cold because  you did all this damage when the  business was hot so now when you're  trying to bring it back up by actually  doing better stuff you've already lost a  chunk of your audience they bailed after  giving you that money you know back when  you were hot and you thought everything  was fine and I think Justice League is  suffering that suffering from that to a  certain extent because you know like I  said there were mixed to bad  receptions to Man of Steel Batman V  Superman and Suicide Squad and I know  some people like to tell you well  obviously audiences liked it much more  than critics except that at least  anecdotally in my experience audiences  didn't really like some people some  people did really like both but in terms  of the percentages I don't think it's as  far off as the defenders of those  earlier films like to claim that they  are I do think that by and large  audiences were giving DC the benefit of  the doubt with Man of Steel through  Batman V Superman through Suicide Squad  but then by then they were kind of going  oh I don't know anymore  and the question then becomes well why  didn't Wonder Woman build it back up and  the thing is the first thing to remember  is Wonder Woman though we did open  bigger than Justice League not by much  that opened I think at 103 which was  ahead of its expectations but the main  reason that Wonder Woman who was a big  success was that it had legs it did not  have a big drop Weekend over weekend and  that you can chalk up largely to  word-of-mouth people saying oh it's  really good and then people going so  that explains why Wonder Woman has been  such a success but why didn't that carry  over why didn't that help make Justice  League a big success well because  justice Lee  just tied up with a lot of baggage from  the movie that people didn't like so  much and that's Batman V Superman you've  got that Batman back you've got that  director back yes I know he was removed  just we can finish but his name's still  on it and you can still see his  fingerprints all over it  and it's an it's a direct continuation  of that story so Wonder Woman in many  ways is anomalous because it is the most  removed from the other films than the DC  EU so far it has the fewest connections  it's the most standalone so on the one  hand that means that Wonder Woman is not  as dragged down by the baggage of that  Cinematic Universe having not really  connected yet it also means that it  being successful doesn't buoy the rest  of that cinematic universe the way that  it might have otherwise and so that's  sort of my assessment on what happened  so the question is what's next well what  I'm hoping is that the folks at Warner  Brothers will realize sort of the  delayed negative box-office reaction to  what they've been doing that they kind  of already knew people weren't going for  and they have already made changes and  and and I said this in my review Justice  League very much feels like a middle  step course Craig it feels like the  pivot move and they're not they had they  didn't have the ability to completely  change it into what they now think they  want to alter this universe to be but it  is also a step away from what it was  that wasn't working so it's it's the  pivot move and then theoretically next  movies we start actually getting things  pointing towards where a warner brothers  and and the folks at DC have decided  where it's gonna go and I'm hoping that  they basically accept Justice League as  a loss leader that it is a movie that is  not going to do as well because of the  damage done by the earlier films but to  not take it under performing as meaning  oh this new direction doesn't work I  hope they will think you're okay well  this underperform but that's cuz we have  to build the good will back so that is  my hope that they don't look at this and  go ah the new direction isn't working  either and then they start scrambling  again like they did in the wake of  Batman V Superman I really hope that  isn't the case and I  hopefully they're paying attention to  fan reactions because again not only for  myself but for most of the people who  I've talked with who have seen it most  of them have been if not thrilled with  it not upset by it not disappointed  there's nowhere near so the anger that I  saw thrown at Batman V Superman so I'm  hoping that Warner Brothers has there  has their finger on that pulse so that  they are realizing that this can be a  first start to winning back some  goodwill and building things back up  again it's just if they expect sort of  immediate response and immediate turn on  that's where they're gonna make a dumb  mistake because I mean that's how they  got into this mess cuz you rationalize  thinking what we did this thing and  people are saying it wasn't good but  they paid all this money for it it must  be fine no it takes a little while for  the reaction to actually translate to  dollars both in the negative and in the  positive so hopefully they're gonna take  that into account and that's kind of why  I'm hoping they're gonna scrap the idea  of doing a the flash movie as flashpoint  because the obvious reason to do that is  to be able to do major changes to this  universe with an in continuity  explanation I don't I don't think that's  necessary anymore I think they've shown  with this movie that they can adjust the  universe as they are already making it  and the sort of hard changes that  flashpoint or that sort of reboot would  mean I don't think are actually  necessary though and the main thing that  I think they need to do is they need to  de-emphasize the the continuity honestly  they need to you know get good directors  onto these things have them at best be  doing continuity nods but not have films  where the primary thing is setting up  what comes next because regardless of  what you think of a story of Batman V  Superman a big part of its mandate in  terms of what it was supposed to  accomplish as a film was to directly set  up justice league and Wonder Woman and  all this other stuff to come after and  you know if you're looking at the Marvel  movies yes they usually set things up  but it would be in limited doses it  would be like here's this thread and  we'll pick that thread up in the next  movie but that  that stuff was also usually fairly  removable they didn't try to integrate  it directly into the story and how to  drive the plot the way setting up  justice league and other things tried to  do a Batman V Superman which is why that  story just doesn't come together it's  trying to do too many things at the same  time it was also one of the problems  with the amazing spider-man 2 I really  hope that they just refocus on what it  should have been in the first place the  individual characters the individual  franchises get them in the hands of good  directors good writers people with  vision and then leave them alone and  don't overly dictate what they need to  do to tie everything together and I say  that about DC and I don't say that about  Marvel because at this point Marvel has  very very clearly has a fairly  well-oiled 